.TH SYSLOGCEF 1 "August 2026" "syslogcef 0.1.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
syslogcef \- convert syslog events to ArcSight Common Event Format (CEF)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B syslogcef
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATHS\fR...]
.br
.B syslogcef
.BI \-\-listen " PROTO:PORT"
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B syslogcef
converts syslog events into ArcSight Common Event Format (CEF) for
consumption by SIEM platforms. It reads from files, standard input, or
the network, and writes CEF records to standard output, a file, a
remote SIEM, or a Kafka topic.
.PP
Ten input formats are detected automatically: RFC3164 (BSD, with or
without a year in the timestamp), RFC5424 (including structured data),
rsyslog JSON and file formats, systemd journal exports (JSON, short,
and ISO), native Cisco console output (sequence numbers and clock-state
markers), ISO-timestamp syslog with PRI, and key=value streams
(Fortinet FortiGate, Sophos XG). Lines matching none of these are
analyzed adaptively: a timestamp shape and host candidate are located
and a pattern for the layout is synthesized and cached. Unparseable
lines never abort the stream; they fall back to a raw-message event.
.PP
Vendor mappings for Cisco ASA/IOS, F5 BIG-IP, Fortinet, Sophos,
VMware, and generic Linux are bundled and selected automatically from
message content. Log content is treated as untrusted input: CEF header
and extension values are escaped so crafted messages cannot forge
header fields or split records.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.I PATHS
Input files. When omitted and
.B \-\-listen
is not given, lines are read from standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-tail
Follow the input files like
.BR "tail \-f" ;
all given files are tailed concurrently.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " PROTO:PORT"
Receive syslog over the network instead of reading files or standard
input. Accepts
.BR udp: \fIPORT\fR,
.BR tcp: \fIPORT\fR,
or
.IB PROTO : HOST : PORT
to bind a specific address (default: all interfaces). TCP accepts
multiple concurrent connections with newline-delimited messages.
Overrides \fIPATHS\fR and
.BR \-\-tail .
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " MODE"
Force a specific parser instead of auto-detection. One of
.BR rfc3164 ,
.BR rfc5424 ,
.BR rsyslog_json ,
.BR rsyslog_file ,
.BR journald_json ,
.BR journald_short ,
.BR journald_iso ,
.BR cisco_seq ,
.BR iso_syslog ,
.BR kv .
.SS Conversion
.TP
.BI \-\-mapping " FILE"
Use the mapping JSON file \fIFILE\fR. When omitted, a bundled mapping
is selected automatically from message content.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check CEF extension values against the ArcSight extension dictionary
(known keys, data types, port ranges, IP/MAC/timestamp formats,
maximum string lengths). Violations are logged as warnings; conversion
continues. Unknown keys are advisory only, as the CEF standard permits
custom keys.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Like
.BR \-\-validate ,
but exit with status 1 on the first type or length violation.
.TP
.B \-\-multiprocess
Convert lines using a process pool.
.TP
.BI \-\-pool\-size " N"
Worker count for
.B \-\-multiprocess
(default: CPU count minus one).
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write CEF lines to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output. Flushed per
line in
.B \-\-tail
and
.B \-\-listen
modes.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-append
Append to the output file instead of truncating it. Used by the system
service so restarts never lose archived events.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " URL"
Forward each CEF record to a remote destination:
.RS
.TP
.BI udp:// HOST : PORT
one datagram per record.
.TP
.BI tcp:// HOST : PORT
newline-delimited stream with automatic reconnect and backoff.
.TP
.BI kafka:// BROKER : PORT / TOPIC
produce to a Kafka topic; requires the optional dependency
(\fBpip install syslog2cef[kafka]\fR).
.RE
.IP
Combinable with
.B \-\-output
to archive locally while forwarding, and with
.B \-\-listen
for a complete receive\-convert\-forward daemon.
.TP
.BI \-\-eps " N"
Rate-limit
.B \-\-send
to at most \fIN\fR events per second.
.SS General
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Python logging level (default
.BR WARNING ).
Validation findings are emitted at WARNING.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Strict validation failed, an input file could not be read, or a
network destination was unreachable after retries.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a log file to CEF on standard output:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef < /var/log/syslog
.RE
.fi
.PP
Convert several files into one archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/messages /var/log/secure \-o events.cef
.RE
.fi
.PP
Follow two files in real time, appending CEF to an archive:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/asa.log /var/log/messages \-\-tail \-\-append \-o events.cef
.RE
.fi
.PP
Act as a syslog receiver and forward CEF to an ArcSight connector:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef \-\-listen udp:514 \-\-send tcp://siem.example.com:514
.RE
.fi
.PP
Receive on a high port (no privileges needed), archive locally, and
forward rate-limited to 5000 events per second:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef \-\-listen tcp:5514 \-\-append \-o /var/log/syslogcef/events.cef \\
          \-\-send udp://siem.example.com:514 \-\-eps 5000
.RE
.fi
.PP
Stream converted events into Kafka:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef /var/log/messages \-\-tail \-\-send kafka://broker.example.com:9092/cef\-events
.RE
.fi
.PP
Force the Cisco ASA mapping and validate the output strictly:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef asa.log \-\-mapping /etc/syslogcef/mapping.json \-\-strict
.RE
.fi
.PP
High-volume batch conversion with a process pool:
.PP
.nf
.RS
syslogcef big.log \-\-multiprocess \-\-pool\-size 8 \-o big.cef
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/syslogcef/syslogcef.conf
Configuration for the system service (environment file): input file or
network endpoints, output file, and extra arguments. To run the
service as a network daemon, leave INPUT_FILE empty and set, for
example, EXTRA_ARGS="\-\-listen udp:514 \-\-send tcp://siem:514".
.TP
.I syslogcef.service
systemd unit (RPM and Debian packages). It carries
CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE so ports below 1024 can be bound without running
as root. On Alpine the equivalent OpenRC service is configured via
.IR /etc/conf.d/syslogcef .
.TP
.I syslogcef.mappings
Bundled mapping files, importable from the Python package
(\fBfrom syslogcef.mappings import load_mapping\fR).
.SH SECURITY
All log content is treated as untrusted. Pipes and backslashes are
escaped in CEF header fields (CR/LF stripped) and pipes, equals signs,
backslashes, and CR/LF are escaped in extension values, preventing
header forgery and record splitting. The listener decodes input as
UTF-8 with replacement and never evaluates message content. Report
vulnerabilities privately; see the SECURITY.md file in the source
distribution.
